  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn open wave trough over GA/SC will move up off the coast of\nVA/NC today resulting in the chance (15-30%) for light rain/drizzle\nacross the mountains today. Higher chance for precipitation \nwill be in the southern mountains of West Virginia and the \nGreenbrier Valley of Pocahontas County. Rainfall amounts across \nthese areas are forecast to only total a few hundredths of an \ninch. \n\nA cold front then crosses late Sunday into Monday with a west to\neast moving swath of light rain/drizzle accompanying its passage\nMonday morning. Rainfall amounts of a few hundredths of an inch\nto a couple tenths of an inch will be likely across the area\nMonday morning.\n\nBehind this front, there will be a return of dry and mostly \nclear skies and colder temperatures Monday night and Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOur mid week/Thanksgiving storm system still remains somewhat of\nan enigma. A large low pressure center drops out of the \nnorthern plains late Wednesday and interacts with a deepening \ntrough that will move up from the Deep South. Come Thursday, the\nforecast area is slated to be sandwiched in the split flow \nbetween the northern plains low taking direct aim at our region \nand the southern low moving up along the east coast of the \nCONUS, eventually becoming a deep, negative-tilt trough. \n\nThe models still differ on timing and positioning of these\nsystems, which will overall affect the weather type and \nimpacts. At this point, the winter weather chance for Wednesday\nand Thanksgiving Day has certainly backed off substantially \nfrom previous runs. Currently, models show mostly rain and windy\nconditions across our forecast area. Still, with colder \ntemperatures in place across the northeastern mountains ahead of\nthis system could lead to a possible mix of rain or snow across\nthe highest elevations Wednesday night into Thanksgiving.\n\nModels are however starting to hone in on the possibility for \nbetter snow chances across the mountains Thanksgiving night and \nFriday. However, given previous divergence and wavering solution\nwith the models there is low confidence at this time until we \ndraw near to the timeframe. \n\nNonetheless, Thanksgiving Day and into the holiday weekend looks\nto be active with likelihood of soaking rain, cool temperatures, \nand windy conditions across the higher elevations.",
